For this one I built Marché, a small storefront that pulls live product data from the Fake Store API and lets you search, filter by category, add things to a cart, and actually check out — no real payment, but the whole flow works end to end. The biggest technical decision was reusing the exact same Button, Card, Modal, and Toast functions from the Week 5 UI kit instead of rebuilding them, which meant the storefront's "add to cart" toast and the "order placed" modal came almost for free once the components existed. I went with hash-based routing again for the product detail and checkout pages, kyunke by this point it's become my default pattern — one HTML file, no page reloads, and the cart state stays alive across every route since it's just a JS variable backed by localStorage. The trickiest part was keeping the cart in sync in three places at once — the grid's Add button, the product detail page, and the drawer itself — so I centralized every mutation through addToCart()/setQty()/removeFromCart() functions that always save to storage and re-render the drawer, instead of letting each UI spot update the cart independently. If I had more time I'd add persistent cart syncing across devices (right now it's just this one browser), a "recently viewed" section using the same card component, and probably debounce the search input instead of filtering on every keystroke. I'd also want real inventory/stock checking, since right now you could add 500 of something that doesn't actually exist in that quantity. Testing this one taught me the most about separating concerns — I actually caught a bug where a rendering failure could get mislabeled as a network error, which wouldn't have been obvious without deliberately testing the failure paths, not just the happy path.
